ZERO WEB 0.15 PREVIEW
25 November 2026

An original document engine and native GUI written entirely in Zero. Chromium,
WebKit, V8, a Python interpreter and external GUI toolkits are not included.
Ezero remains the project's easier language built on Zero; this engine is Zero.
External HTML, CSS and JavaScript are interoperability inputs, not project
implementation languages. A website's server-side Python does not require a
Python interpreter in the browser.

This is a local-document preview, not a finished modern Internet browser.
The previous Zero OS HTTP text reader remains available in the system. This
new engine is currently a Linux/Windows program and is not installed in the
running Zero OS kernel. The system image itself remains the verified0.14base.

Run
  zero-web INPUT
  zero-web --render INPUT NEW.zpix
  zero-web --inspect INPUT
  zero-web --check INPUT
Windows uses zero-web.exe. INPUT is a regular external HTML file or our own
ZTEXT001 container containing HTML. demo.ztext is included. --render creates
an original ZPIX0001 image exclusively and refuses an existing destination.
--inspect prints sanitized ASCII text; --check validates the supported profile.
Warnings identify unsupported features; acceptance never means full web support.

GUI
The native800x600window paints an original RGB canvas and own bitmap font.
Up/Down, PageUp/PageDown, Home/End and mouse wheel scroll; Esc or the window
close button exits. Linux uses an original X11 wire client (Xwayland works).
Windows uses Win32 host interfaces. There is no imported toolkit or font.

Engine
HTML is strict UTF-8, at most65536bytes and512nodes. Element parentage,
id/class/style, entities, raw script/style and title/textarea RCDATA are parsed.
Oversized attributes and unsupported executable contexts are rejected.
CSS supports simple tag/class/id/universal selectors, source order/specificity,
inline styles, inherited color/font-size, color/background-color, display,
one-value padding/margin and width. Unsupported syntax is skipped with a warning.
JavaScript supports a bounded subset: let/const/var, assignments, nonnegative
safe integers, strings, addition/concatenation and getElementById(...).textContent.
Failed script parsing rejects the whole page before GUI/output publication.
There are no file, process, eval, import or network APIs available to scripts.

Layout is a bounded block/inline flow preview with BR breaks, ASCII word wrap,
character fallback for long words, block and inline text backgrounds, margin,
padding and width. Fonts use an integer-scaled original5x7glyph set; supported
glyphs are ASCII. Other Unicode scalars display as question marks. This is not
CSS box-layout conformance, shaping, browser-standard font metrics or HTML5
tree-building recovery. Read DOM.ztext, CSS.ztext and JAVASCRIPT.ztext for details.

Not implemented in this new engine
URL/network fetching, TLS/HTTPS, external styles/scripts, full ECMAScript,
DOM events, forms, flex/grid, image formats, multimedia, cookies/storage,
WebAssembly, accessibility tree, complex fonts and guest-app integration.
All-sites compatibility and a complete Chromium rewrite are not claimed.

Build from original source
The raw original compiler accepts source on stdin and writes native bytes:
  zero --host < browser/engine/compose.zero > zero-web-compose
  zero-web-compose PROJECT NEW_WEB.zero
  zero --host < NEW_WEB.zero > zero-web
Use --windows for a Windows executable. Set the executable bit on Linux.
The standard zero-build now emits72artifacts in76stages, including zero-web,
zero-web-compose and zero-web-check for Linux and Windows. The own compiler
fixed point is verified first. No foreign compiler, linker or bundler is used.

Own checks
The composer also accepts --dom-check, --css-check, --javascript-check,
--engine-check and --gui-check as the final argument, producing original Zero
test programs. Compiler/runtime and source modules remain shared with the
project. Execution evidence and limitations are recorded in VERIFICATION.ztext.

Next work
Integrate the engine with network fetching and the guest runtime, implement
TLS using original code, extend DOM/ECMAScript and CSS compatibility, add own
media decoders and scalable text. These are plans, not present capabilities.
